@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+# Deploying the Tip Jar site to IPFS
+
+The site ships as a static export (`packages/nextjs/out`) that targets **real Base** (chain id 8453). It has no server; every page is a pre-rendered HTML file.
+
+Run the commands below from the repo root unless a step says otherwise. Replace `<...>` placeholders.
+
+## 0. Prerequisite: TipJar deployed on Base
+
+The static site reads the TipJar address for chain 8453 from `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts`. **This step spends real ETH on Base; get sign-off first.**
+
+```bash
+yarn account:import                                      # or: yarn generate (fund the address with ETH on Base)
+TIP_RECIPIENT=<recipient-address> yarn deploy --network base
+```
+
+Confirm that the Base entry exists and note the address:
+
+```bash
+grep -A3 "8453: {" packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts
+# expect:  8453: {  TipJar: {  address: "0x..."
+```
+
+If there is no `8453` entry, the site builds fine but shows "contract not deployed". Do not ship it in that state.
+
+## 1. Build
+
+Set the production origin (the URL people will open; it's used for Open Graph/Twitter metadata). Use your own RPC and WalletConnect keys: the defaults are shared, rate-limited keys, and env vars are baked into the static bundle at build time.
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+rm -rf .next out
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://<name>.eth.limo" \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY="<alchemy-key>" \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID="<walletconnect-project-id>" \
+  yarn build:ipfs
+```
+
+`build:ipfs` sets `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true`, which in `next.config.ts` turns on `output: "export"`, `trailingSlash: true` (one `route/index.html` per page) and unoptimized images, and switches the target network to Base. The build fails if `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` is missing. `NODE_OPTIONS=--no-experimental-webstorage` is applied by the build script, which works around the Node 25 `localStorage.getItem is not a function` crash during prerender.
+
+## 2. Verify before upload
+
+Still in `packages/nextjs`:
+
+```bash
+# Every route has its own index.html (expect /, 404, blockexplorer, debug)
+ls out/index.html out/*/index.html
+
+# Metadata uses the production origin, not localhost
+grep -o '<meta property="og:\(url\|image\)" content="[^"]*"' out/index.html
+grep -rl "localhost:3000" out && echo "❌ localhost leaked into build" || echo "✅ no localhost"
+
+# The Base TipJar address and Base USDC are in the bundle
+TIPJAR=$(grep -A3 "8453: {" contracts/deployedContracts.ts | grep -o '0x[0-9a-fA-F]\{40\}' | head -1)
+[ -n "$TIPJAR" ] && grep -rlq "$TIPJAR" out/_next/static && echo "✅ TipJar $TIPJAR in bundle" || echo "❌ TipJar missing"
+grep -rlq "0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913" out/_next/static && echo "✅ USDC in bundle"
+
+# Serve it the way a gateway will (at the origin root) and click through
+python3 -m http.server 8788 --directory out   # open http://localhost:8788, wallet on Base
+```
+
+## 3. Upload
+
+Uses the BuidlGuidl IPFS CLI (`bgipfs`, already a dev dependency). You need a BGIPFS API key.
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+yarn bgipfs upload config init -u https://upload.bgipfs.com -k <BGIPFS_API_KEY>   # one time
+yarn bgipfs upload out
+# → prints "CID: <cid>". Record it with the git commit you built from:
+git rev-parse HEAD
+```
+
+If the CID is the same as the previous release, the bytes you uploaded are identical. Check the build output and the directory you uploaded before blaming gateway caching.
+
+## 4. Verify after upload
+
+Use a **subdomain** gateway (`<cid>.ipfs.<gateway>`). Next.js loads assets from root-absolute `/_next/...` paths, which only resolve when the site sits at the origin root, as it does on subdomain gateways and eth.limo. Path-style URLs (`/ipfs/<cid>/`) return the HTML but break the scripts.
+
+```bash
+CID=<cid>
+GW="https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link"
+
+# Root and non-home routes (expect 200 for each, not 404)
+for p in / /debug/ /blockexplorer/; do
+  curl -s -o /dev/null -w "$p %{http_code}\n" -L "$GW$p"
+done
+
+# A JS chunk referenced by the page loads (expect 200)
+ASSET=$(curl -sL "$GW/" | grep -o '/_next/static/[^"]*\.js' | head -1)
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "$ASSET %{http_code}\n" -L "$GW$ASSET"
+
+# Open Graph URL and image point at the production origin
+curl -sL "$GW/" | grep -o '<meta property="og:\(url\|image\)" content="[^"]*"'
+```
+
+Then open `$GW` in a browser with a wallet on Base. Confirm that the network shown is Base, the tip feed loads, and "Tips go to" shows the expected recipient. Sending a real tip (for example 0.01 USDC) is optional and spends real money.
+
+## 5. ENS (optional)
+
+Only after the CID above has been reviewed and approved: in the ENS app, set the name's content hash to `ipfs://<cid>`. Then:
+
+```bash
+curl -sI "https://<name>.eth.limo/" | grep -i "x-ipfs-path"      # expect /ipfs/<cid>/
+for p in / /debug/; do curl -s -o /dev/null -w "$p %{http_code}\n" -L "https://<name>.eth.limo$p"; done
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "og image %{http_code}\n" "https://<name>.eth.limo/thumbnail.jpg"
+```
+
+The release is done only when all of the checks above pass.
+
+## Known limitation
+
+The feed uses `useScaffoldEventHistory`, which scans `Tip` logs from the deployment block in 500-block batches. Base produces a block every 2s, so the scan grows by about 43k blocks (roughly 90 RPC calls) per day after deployment. That's fine for a demo. For long-lived production use, move the feed to an indexer (Ponder or a subgraph).
